-- title:
--   Theorem B.5.2.1(1) (Kronecker–Weyl) — the closure of a one-parameter subgroup of $\mathbb T^N$ is a torus of dimension $\dim_{\mathbb Q}\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb Q}\{\tau_1,\dots,\tau_N\}$
-- statement:
--   **Theorem B.5.2.1(1) (Kronecker–Weyl): the orbit closure is a torus of the expected dimension.** Let $N\ge 0$, $\mathbb T=\mathbb R/\mathbb Z$, let $\tau=(\tau_1,\dots,\tau_N)\in\mathbb R^N$ and let $\overline\gamma_\tau\colon\mathbb R\to\mathbb T^N$, $\overline\gamma_\tau(t)=(t\tau_1,\dots,t\tau_N)\bmod\mathbb Z^N$, be the continuous one-parameter subgroup generated by $\tau$. Let
--   $$d=\dim_{\mathbb Q}\bigl(\mathbb Q\tau_1+\dots+\mathbb Q\tau_N\bigr),\qquad 0\le d\le N,$$
--   be the dimension of the $\mathbb Q$-linear subspace of $\mathbb R$ generated by the coordinates of $\tau$, and let $G=\overline{\overline\gamma_\tau(\mathbb R)}$ be the closure of the image of $\overline\gamma_\tau$. Then $G$, which is a closed subgroup of $\mathbb T^N$ (the closure of a subgroup), is isomorphic as a topological group to a $d$-dimensional torus:
--   $$G\;\simeq\;\mathbb T^{d}.$$
--
--   In particular $G=\mathbb T^N$ when the $\tau_k$ are $\mathbb Q$-linearly independent ($d=N$), and $G=\{0\}$ when $\tau=0$ ($d=0$). The torus $G$ with its normalized Haar measure $\mu_G$ is the space on which the flow $t\mapsto\overline\gamma_\tau(t)$ equidistributes (part (2) of the theorem, eq. (B.5.2.1)); the source applies this with $\tau_v=\log(q_v)/2\pi$ for the finite places $v$ of a finite set $S$ (proof of Proposition B.5.3.3), where the $\tau_v$ may be $\mathbb Q$-linearly dependent.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The index set $\{1,\dots,N\}$ is an arbitrary finite type `ι` (so $N=0$ is allowed). $\mathbb T$ is Mathlib's `UnitAddCircle = AddCircle (1 : ℝ)`, the coercion `ℝ → UnitAddCircle` is the quotient map $\mathbb R\to\mathbb R/\mathbb Z$, and $\mathbb T^N$ is `ι → UnitAddCircle` (Mathlib's `UnitAddTorus ι`) with the product topology; $\overline\gamma_\tau(t)$ is written inline as `fun k => ((t * τ k : ℝ) : UnitAddCircle)`. The orbit closure is represented by an additive subgroup `G` of `UnitAddTorus ι` together with the hypothesis `hG` that its underlying set is the closure of the range of $\overline\gamma_\tau$ (exactly one such subgroup exists, the closure of a subgroup being a subgroup). "$d$-dimensional closed torus" is expressed by the existence of an isomorphism of topological groups `G ≃ₜ+ UnitAddTorus (Fin d)` with `d = Module.finrank ℚ (Submodule.span ℚ (Set.range τ))`.
-- source:
--   A. M. Masullo, The Music of the Primes, draft of 20 November 2026, https://math216.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/the-music-of-the-primes.pdf, p. 627, Theorem B.5.2.1(1) (Kronecker-Weyl); classical reference: B. Hasselblatt and A. Katok, A First Course in Dynamics. With a Panorama of Recent Developments, Cambridge University Press, 2003, Part 1, Theorem 4.1.15 and §4.1.6 (of which the source's theorem is the multi-dimensional version); P. Humphreys, The Summatory Function of Liouville's Function and Pólya's Conjecture, BPh thesis, Australian National University, 2010, §4.3, proof of Lemma 4.3 (general case, as cited by the source)

import Mathlib

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace KroneckerWeyl

theorem closure_range_torusFlow_equiv_torus {ι : Type*} [Finite ι] (τ : ι → ℝ)
    (G : AddSubgroup (UnitAddTorus ι))
    (hG : (G : Set (UnitAddTorus ι)) =
      closure (Set.range fun t : ℝ => fun k : ι => ((t * τ k : ℝ) : UnitAddCircle))) :
    Nonempty (G ≃ₜ+ UnitAddTorus (Fin (Module.finrank ℚ (Submodule.span ℚ (Set.range τ))))) := by
  sorry

end KroneckerWeyl
